before:
The supplied parsePage helper accepts digit-only text, converts it with Number, and checks Number.isSafeInteger.

```javascript
console.log(Number("12"));
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(12));
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(1.5));
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(9007199254740992));
```
The outputs are 12, true, false, false. Number converts the supplied text to a number. Number.isSafeInteger receives a value and returns whether it is an integer within JavaScript’s safely representable integer range. It does not convert quoted text or validate the allowed page range; those remain separate rules.

The list must have a predictable order before choosing its page. Sort a copied array so the original order remains untouched.
```javascript
const items = [{id: 8}, {id: 2}, {id: 5}];
const ordered = [...items].sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id);
console.log(ordered.map(item => item.id));
```

slice(start, end) includes start but stops before end. For offset 1 and limit 2, use end 3.
```javascript
const names = ["A", "B", "C", "D"];
console.log(names.slice(1, 3));
```

after:
```javascript
const entries = [{id: 8}, {id: 2}, {id: 5}];
const offset = 1;
const limit = 2;
const ordered = [...entries].sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id);
const page = ordered.slice(offset, offset + limit);
console.log(page.map(entry => entry.id));
console.log(entries.map(entry => entry.id));
```
This prints [5, 8], then [8, 2, 5]. The page uses sorted order, while the original array stays unchanged.
